# Firmware Updater — environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to .env (for local dev) and adjust as needed.
# See README.md "Safety status" section before changing VITE_ENABLE_REAL_FLASHING.

# Master safety switch for sending real firmware-update commands to hardware
# over Web Serial. Must remain "false" until the protocol has been validated
# against a physical device (see docs/gtool-analysis for open items).
#
# In this phase, the Web Serial transport does not implement firmware
# transmission at all (see src/lib/webserial), so real flashing is disabled
# in two independent places. This flag is the single documented switch a
# future phase will flip once hardware validation is complete.
VITE_ENABLE_REAL_FLASHING=false

# Phase 2A: read-only device connection switch. When "true", the "Connect
# device" button on a supported browser will request a real serial port,
# open it, and send ONLY the recovered harmless version-query command
# (docs/gtool-analysis/protocol_section.js "ge()") to read back the
# installed firmware version. No firmware-update initialization command and
# no FE EF firmware packet can ever be sent through this path — see
# src/lib/webserial/ReadOnlyDeviceConnection.ts, which has no method capable
# of sending arbitrary bytes, and README.md "Phase 2A" for the full safety
# argument.
#
# This flag is completely independent from VITE_ENABLE_REAL_FLASHING and
# never bypasses it: turning this on does not enable firmware writing.
# Must remain "false" in every deployed build. Enable locally only, via
# .env.local (see README.md "Phase 2A" for a bench-test procedure), never
# in .env or committed configuration.
VITE_ENABLE_READ_ONLY_DEVICE_CONNECTION=false

# Phase 2B: temporary bench-testing gate for real firmware transfer. Real
# firmware writing requires ALL THREE of VITE_ENABLE_READ_ONLY_DEVICE_CONNECTION,
# VITE_ENABLE_REAL_FLASHING, and this flag to be "true" at once — see
# src/lib/webserial/flags.ts `isRealFirmwareTransferEnabled()`, which
# `WebSerialTransport` checks before every real operation. Any one or two of
# the three alone leave firmware writing unreachable.
#
# This flag exists because Phase 2A established that the recovered
# version-query reply carries no device/model name field, so a successful
# reply can never positively confirm a connected device is genuinely a
# PT-SP-HD14-48G (see DeviceIdentityResult.compatible). When all three flags
# are true, the app exposes a clearly labelled "Hardware validation mode"
# bench screen that requires the operator to explicitly acknowledge that
# compatibility could not be electronically confirmed and type the exact
# product name (see src/lib/webserial/hardwareValidation.ts) before the
# real update action becomes reachable.
#
# Must remain "false" in every deployed build. Enable locally only, via
# .env.local, on a bench with a physical PT-SP-HD14-48G — see
# docs/hardware-validation/PT-SP-HD14-48G-bench-test.md.
VITE_ENABLE_HARDWARE_VALIDATION_MODE=false
